540. Single Element in a Sorted Array
You are given a sorted array consisting of only integers where every element appears exactly twice, except for one element which appears exactly once.
Return the single element that appears only once.
Your solution must run in O(log n) time and O(1) space.

Explanation:
To check if it is single element, check if the mid element , the before and after elements are not same.

Here we are using 2 pointers. so left=1; right=n-2;
but before that we need to understand this logic

if you closely observe this array, 
[1,1,2,3,3,4,4,8,8] , [3,3,7,7,10,11,11]
In a sorted array where every element appears twice except one:
  Before the single element, pairs follow the pattern (even, odd)
  After the single element, the pattern shifts to (odd, even)
  The single element is exactly where this pairing pattern breaks.

At each step, we choose a middle index and:
  First check if it is the single element by comparing it with its neighbors
  Otherwise, we verify whether the pairing pattern still holds at mid

The pairing pattern is valid if:
  mid is even and nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1], or
  mid is odd and nums[mid] == nums[mid - 1]

If this condition holds, the single element must be on the right side, so we eliminate the left half.
If it does not hold, the single element lies on the left side, so we eliminate the right half.

For edge cases, we need to check if the first 2 elements are not same and last 2 elements are not same.


Code:

class Solution {
    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int n=nums.length;
        // Edge case of 1 element present
        if(n==1){
            return nums[0];
        }

        //Edge case of first 2 elements
        if(nums[0]!=nums[1]){
            return nums[0];
        }

        // Edge case of last 2 elements
        if(nums[n-1]!=nums[n-2]){
            return nums[n-1];
        }

        // 2 pointers
        int left=1; int right=n-2;
        while(left<=right){
            int mid=(left+right)/2;

            if(nums[mid]!=nums[mid-1] && nums[mid]!=nums[mid+1]){
                return nums[mid];
            }

            // (even,odd) (odd,even) to find if the logic hold true
            if( (mid%2==0 && nums[mid]==nums[mid+1]) || (mid%2==1 && nums[mid]==nums[mid-1]) ){
                left=mid+1;
            }
            else{
                right=mid-1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
